<rml>
<head>
<style>
body {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    top: 0px;
    left: 0px;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    font-family: "Rajdhani";
    font-weight: 600;
}
#scrim {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    /* The sketch uses a radial-gradient here (circle at 80% 20%) — true
       radial gradients go through RenderManager::CompileShader, which
       CgfsRmlRenderInterface doesn't implement (confirmed via grep, and via
       the DLL log: "Could not generate decorator element data" firing every
       frame). vertical-gradient is a safe, close approximation: confirmed
       (DecoratorGradient.cpp's DecoratorStraightGradient::RenderElement)
       that horizontal-gradient/vertical-gradient render as plain
       vertex-colored geometry — no CompileShader call at all — so they
       already work with this renderer's existing, proven RenderGeometry
       path, unlike every other gradient function. */
    decorator: vertical-gradient(#1A103CE6 #080B12F2);
}
#panel {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    background-color: #0C101CD9;
    border: 1px #FFFFFF1F;
    border-radius: 16px;
    /* Real glow, Phase B (blur) now implemented — kept modest (20px blur =
       10px sigma = 30px kernel radius, comfortably under this renderer's
       BLUR_MAX_RADIUS=40) rather than the sketch's original 60px blur,
       both to stay well inside the kernel's supported range and to avoid
       the oversized-shadow-texture-vs-window warning a full-panel-sized
       shadow with a very large blur radius triggered earlier in this
       phase (see the plan's box-shadow investigation notes). */
    box-shadow: 0px 8px 20px #000000A6;
}
#panel-accent {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    /* Sketch's --grad-primary, exact stops — horizontal-gradient is the
       safe, shader-free decorator (see #scrim's comment). */
    decorator: horizontal-gradient(#00F2FE #4FACFE);
    box-shadow: 0px 0px 10px #00F2FE99;
    /* cubic-bezier(0.25, 1, 0.5, 1) isn't valid RCSS syntax — same issue
       already hit and fixed on #tab-glider: the transition value-list
       splitter is comma-based and not parenthesis-aware, so the commas
       inside cubic-bezier(...) tear the declaration apart, and RCSS has no
       arbitrary-bezier support in the first place, only named tween
       keywords. cubic-out is the closest match. Only actually visible on a
       window resize (the one thing that changes this element's `width` —
       SetRectPx writes it every frame, but only a resize ever changes the
       *value*, so this smooths that instead of snapping). */
    transition: all 0.3s cubic-out;
}
/* Bottom-right resize handle (drag to resize the panel like a real window —
   see g_panelUserPositioned/g_resizeGrip in cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp, which
   gives this element `drag: drag` and positions it every frame tucked into
   #panel's own bottom-right corner). Deliberately plain/understated at rest
   (a faint tinted square) with a brighter hover state as the only
   affordance it's interactive — same "hover to reveal" language as
   .hint-item.hint-close and .tab elsewhere in this file. The corner
   radius echoes #panel's own 16px bottom-right rounding. */
#resize-grip {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 3px 0px 13px 0px;
    background-color: #FFFFFF1A;
    transition: background-color 0.2s sine-in-out;
}
#resize-grip:hover {
    background-color: #4FACFE40;
}
/* Matches overlay.html's .brand-title/.brand-badge values exactly —
   font-size/letter-spacing/padding/transform/gradient/box-shadow all now
   render for real (Phase B implemented box-shadow blur). */
#brand-title {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 12px;
    position: absolute;
}
.brand-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: "Teko";
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 38px;
    letter-spacing: 2px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    line-height: 1;
    color: #FFFFFFFF;
}
.brand-badge {
    display: block;
    decorator: horizontal-gradient(#FF0844 #FFB199);
    border-radius: 4px;
    padding: 2px 10px;
    font-family: "Teko";
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 18px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    transform: skewX(-12deg);
    color: #FFFFFFFF;
    box-shadow: 0px 0px 12px #FF084480;
}
#tabstrip {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    position: absolute;
    background-color: #00000059;
    border-radius: 8px;
}
#tab-glider {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    decorator: horizontal-gradient(#00F2FE #4FACFE);
    border-radius: 4px;
    /* Matches overlay.html's .tab-glider exactly — this element (not .tab,
       which paints no background of its own) is the visible colored shape
       behind the active tab, so IT needs its own skew to read as a
       parallelogram; missed on the first port, only .tab/.tab-label had it. */
    transform: skewX(-12deg);
    box-shadow: 0px 0px 14px #00F2FE99;
    /* No `transition` here on purpose (a prior version declared one on
       left/width — deleted, it never actually did anything): RCSS only
       starts a transition from ElementStyle::TransitionPropertyChanges,
       which fires on a STYLESHEET DEFINITION change (a class/pseudo-class
       toggle causing different RCSS rules to match) — never for a plain
       inline SetProperty() call, which is exactly what
       cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp's SetRectPx does every time this element needs
       to move. Same root cause, same fix direction, as #panel's own
       g_panelOpenAnimActive comment: the slide is driven by a manual
       per-frame tween in C++ instead (see ApplyTabGliderAnim there), not
       by RCSS at all. */
}
.tab {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    /* flex-basis 0% (not auto) so all tabs land on the exact same equal
       width regardless of label length ("Scoreboards" vs "Kits") — auto
       would size each tab's flex-basis off its own content first, then
       grow, so tabs with shorter labels ended up narrower than
       cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp's #tab-glider (which is always an exact
       tabStripW/NUM_MENU_TABS equal split): the glider and the tab box it
       sits under didn't line up, which is what read as the label not being
       centered on it. */
    flex: 1 1 0%;
    /* position:relative + z-index needed so the tab (in-flow) paints above
       #tab-glider (position:absolute, z-index:auto) despite coming later in
       the DOM — without an explicit stacking order, an absolutely-positioned
       element does not reliably paint behind in-flow siblings just because
       it appears first in markup. Mirrors the sketch's own .tab-btn
       {position:relative; z-index:2} / .tab-glider {z-index:1} split. */
    position: relative;
    z-index: 2;
    color: #8B9BB4FF;
    transform: skewX(-12deg);
    border-radius: 4px;
    /* RCSS has no "ease" tween keyword (CSS's own name for it) — the
       nearest equivalents are the named keywords in
       PropertyParserAnimation.cpp's table; sine-in-out is a gentle,
       symmetric ease-in-out, matching the sketch's `transition: color
       0.25s ease` intent as closely as this renderer's keyword set allows. */
    transition: color 0.2s sine-in-out;
}
/* Dark text follows the glider, not a static :hover/.active rule — see
   RmlMenu_Sync's hoveredTab/gliderTab computation in cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp,
   which toggles this class on whichever tab the glider is CURRENTLY under
   (the hovered tab if any, else the real active tab) every frame. Ports
   overlay.html's `.tab-btn:hover` / `.tabs-wrapper:not(:hover) .tab-btn.
   active` / `.tabs-wrapper:hover .tab-btn.active:not(:hover)` three-rule
   combo into one C++-driven class, since RCSS combinator support for that
   exact "active-unless-something-else-is-hovered" logic isn't something to
   depend on — the same information is already computed in C++ every frame
   for the glider's own position, so driving the text color from the same
   computation keeps both perfectly in sync by construction. */
.tab.on-glider { color: #080B12FF; }
.tab-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: "Teko";
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 24px;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    transform: skewX(12deg);
}
#content-bg {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    /* Deliberately no background-color: #panel's own glass background
       already covers this whole area — a second, slightly-different dark
       fill here (the pre-redesign behavior) is what made the tab strip and
       the content below it read as two stacked boxes instead of one
       cohesive card. Only a hairline divider remains. */
    border-top: 1px #FFFFFF14;
}
#dashboard {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    position: absolute;
    /* Same reasoning as #content-bg above — no separate fill, just a
       divider, so the dashboard band doesn't look like a bolted-on
       different-colored box. */
    border-top: 1px #FFFFFF1F;
    padding: 0px 10px;
}
#dash-text-col {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    row-gap: 6px;
}
/* Right-hand scoreboard widget — crest/score/crest with the match clock
   underneath, shown next to (not instead of) #dash-text-col's stat lines.
   flex-shrink:0 so #dash-text-col (flex:1 1 auto) is what gives way if the
   panel gets narrow, not this. Visibility toggled wholesale by C++ (same
   signal that already gated the crests individually pre-redesign: at least
   one crest path resolved) rather than left to show a bare "0 x 0" with no
   team context. */
#dash-score {
    display: none;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    row-gap: 6px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding-left: 18px;
    /* Pulls the whole widget away from #panel's right edge — plain empty
       space #dash-text-col (flex:1 1 auto) yields into, since #dashboard's
       own width is fixed by C++'s SetRectPx. */
    margin-right: 32px;
}
#dash-score-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 22px;
}
#home-crest, #away-crest {
    display: none;
    width: 76px;
    height: 76px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}
#dash-score-text {
    display: block;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    font-family: "Teko";
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 46px;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    color: #FFFFFFFF;
    min-width: 80px;
    text-align: center;
}
#dash-time-text {
    display: block;
    font-family: "Teko";
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 23px;
    letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    color: #8B9BB4FF;
}
.dashline {
    display: none;
    font-size: 18px;
    letter-spacing: 0.5px;
    color: #D6E0F0FF;
    white-space: nowrap;
}
#wizard-header {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    /* Subtle white-tint overlay rather than its own distinct solid navy
       patch (the pre-redesign color) — reads as a highlighted band within
       the one glass panel, not a separately-colored box stacked on top. */
    background-color: #FFFFFF0F;
    border-bottom: 1px #FFFFFF1F;
    border-radius: 6px;
}
#wizard-header-text {
    display: block;
    padding: 4px 8px;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: #FFCC55FF;
}
#list-area {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    position: absolute;
    overflow: hidden;
}
/* Stadiums country filter bubble state — #list-area is reused as-is (same
   rows, same virtual scroll/click handling) just resized/repositioned into
   a small popover by RmlMenu_Sync while stadium_filter_panel_open is set,
   with this class toggled on top for the "floating box" look. Mirrors
   #panel's own background/border/shadow language (see its rule above). */
#list-area.bubble {
    background-color: #0C101CF2;
    border: 1px #FFCC554D;
    border-radius: 12px;
    box-shadow: 0px 8px 20px #000000A6;
    /* Grid instead of a single vertical stack. How many columns actually
       fit per line is NOT a fixed number — flex-wrap greedily packs as many
       130px-wide cells as the panel's current width allows, and that width
       varies with output resolution/panel size. Two earlier approaches both
       broke navigation as a result: (1) a percentage .row width, which
       under RmlUi's default content-box sizing had .row's own padding-left
       (12px) added on top rather than eaten into, so the real per-column
       footprint didn't reliably match what the fixed COLS constant on the
       Python/C++ side assumed, silently wrapping to fewer columns than
       expected; (2) after switching to this fixed 130px width, hardcoding
       that same wrong assumption (7) elsewhere just moved the mismatch the
       other way — a wide panel fits well more than 7 130px cells per line,
       so a "full row" step sized for 7 landed mid-row instead. Either way
       the symptom was the same: the highlighted cell visibly walked
       diagonally on a straight Up/Down press instead of moving one row.
       The actual fix isn't a better constant — cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp's
       RmlMenu_Sync now computes the real, current column count from this
       same 130px width + 8px column-gap (FILTER_COL_W/FILTER_COL_GAP) and
       reports it to Python each frame (menu_filter_grid_cols /
       get_filter_grid_cols()), so both sides always agree on whatever the
       real count is, at any panel width, instead of guessing a shared
       number up front. This fixed width is the one thing that must stay in
       sync — it's the box-model input that computation is derived from. */
    flex-direction: row;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-content: flex-start;
    column-gap: 8px;
    row-gap: 6px;
}
#list-area.bubble .row {
    width: 130px;
    box-sizing: border-box;
}
.row {
    display: none;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 8px;
    height: 28px;
    font-size: 18px;
    color: #CCD8EAFF;
    padding-left: 12px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background-color: #00000000;
}
.row.selected {
    background-color: #00F2FE29;
    border-left: 3px #00F2FEFF;
}
/* Stadiums country filter bubble only — lit up while a row's country code
   is an active member of the filter (see menu_item_checked /
   RmlOverlay_MenuItemChecked). Deliberately a different side/color than
   .selected (right border, amber) so a row that's both the current cursor
   position AND checked still shows both cues at once. */
.row.checked {
    background-color: #FFCC5522;
    border-right: 3px #FFCC55FF;
}
.row:hover { background-color: #4FACFE1F; }
/* Stadiums tab only — taller row so .row-thumb is actually a recognizable
   photo instead of a color chip; toggled per-row from RmlMenu_Sync only
   while activeTab == Stadiums (see ITEM_H_STADIUMS). */
.row.stadium-row { height: 56px; }
.row-thumb {
    display: none;
    width: 84px;
    height: 47px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}
.row-text {
    display: block;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    overflow: hidden;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
#empty-state {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-size: 18px;
    color: #8B9BB4FF;
}
#scrollbar-track {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    background-color: #0E1522CC;
    border-radius: 6px;
}
#scrollbar-thumb {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    background-color: #4FACFEFF;
    border-radius: 6px;
}
#split-preview {
    display: none;
    flex-direction: column;
    position: absolute;
    background-color: #0F1423E6;
    border: 1px #00F2FE4D;
    border-radius: 12px;
    box-shadow: 0px 0px 20px #00F2FE26;
    padding: 16px;
    row-gap: 12px;
    /* RmlUi defaults to content-box (RCSS's box-sizing default matches
       CSS2.1): without this, `width`/`height` from cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp's
       SetRectPx(g_splitPreview, ...) size the CONTENT box only, and this
       rule's own padding+border get added on top — the box rendered ~17px
       past its intended right/bottom edge each side (34px total), which is
       exactly the "looks like a separate floating window, sticks out past
       the panel" bug. border-box makes width/height mean the full box
       SetRectPx already intends, padding/border included. */
    box-sizing: border-box;
}
#hero-img-wrap {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    position: relative;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    min-height: 0px;
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
    background-color: #00000033;
}
#preview-img {
    display: block;
    max-width: 100%;
    max-height: 100%;
}
#hero-fade {
    display: block;
    position: absolute;
    left: 0px;
    bottom: 0px;
    width: 100%;
    height: 50%;
    /* Top-to-transparent fade over the bottom of the preview image, for
       title legibility — re-added now that horizontal/vertical-gradient are
       confirmed to render for real (plain vertex-colored geometry, unlike
       linear-gradient()/radial-gradient(), see #scrim's comment); dropped
       earlier in this phase when it looked like no gradient function
       rendered at all. */
    decorator: vertical-gradient(#0F142300 #0F1423FF);
}
#hero-title {
    display: block;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    font-family: "Teko";
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 38px;
    letter-spacing: 1px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #FFFFFFFF;
    white-space: nowrap;
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
/* Wraps #hero-btn + #hero-mute-btn side by side — #hero-btn used to be the
   direct flex-column child of #split-preview (stretched full-width by the
   column's default align-items:stretch); now that a second, Movies-only
   button sits beside it, this row takes that slot instead and #hero-btn
   gets flex:1 1 auto (below) to keep filling the remaining width exactly
   like before whenever #hero-mute-btn is hidden (every non-Movies tab). */
#hero-actions-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    column-gap: 8px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
}
#hero-btn {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    column-gap: 8px;
    flex: 1 1 auto;
    /* Sketch's --grad-green, exact stops. */
    decorator: horizontal-gradient(#11998E #38EF7D);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 10px;
    box-shadow: 0px 4px 12px #38EF7D66;
    /* :hover/:active are real RmlUi pseudo-classes here (unlike #tab-glider's
       left/width, these ARE stylesheet-definition changes, so `transition`
       genuinely animates them — see cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp's glider comments
       for the contrasting case where it doesn't). .hero-highlight/.hero-
       pressed are the same two states, just driven by C++ instead, for
       gamepad/keyboard input that never touches a real mouse button — see
       g_heroHighlightCache's comment in cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp. */
    transform: scale(1);
    transition: transform 0.15s cubic-out;
}
#hero-btn:hover, #hero-btn.hero-highlight {
    decorator: horizontal-gradient(#14B39F #45FF97);
    box-shadow: 0px 6px 18px #38EF7DA8;
    transform: scale(1.03);
}
/* Listed after :hover/.hero-highlight so a press while already hovered
   (the normal mouse-click sequence) wins the cascade and shrinks instead
   of staying grown. */
#hero-btn:active, #hero-btn.hero-pressed {
    transform: scale(0.92);
}
.hero-btn-icon {
    display: block;
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
}
.hero-btn-label {
    display: block;
    font-family: "Teko";
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: 24px;
    letter-spacing: 1.5px;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: #08150FFF;
}
/* Movies tab only (see cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp's movieHeroActive) — mutes/
   unmutes the live video's audio, gamepad X per its own field comment in
   RmlMenu_Sync. display:none by default (like #filter-btn below), shown via
   SetVisible only while a video is actually playing — muting a placeholder
   icon makes no sense. Deliberately icon-only (no text label, unlike
   #hero-btn) to stay compact beside it. */
#hero-mute-btn {
    display: none;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    column-gap: 6px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    background-color: #1B2333FF;
    border: 1px #3A4A66FF;
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 10px 14px;
    transition: transform 0.15s cubic-out, background-color 0.15s;
}
#hero-mute-btn:hover, #hero-mute-btn.hero-mute-highlight {
    background-color: #26334AFF;
    border: 1px #4FACFEFF;
    transform: scale(1.03);
}
#hero-mute-btn:active, #hero-mute-btn.hero-mute-pressed {
    transform: scale(0.92);
}
.hero-mute-icon {
    display: block;
    width: 24px;
    height: 24px;
}
/* Small gamepad-X badge beside the mute/unmute state icon — hidden in
   keyboard/mouse mode (click is self-explanatory there), shown in gamepad
   mode as the "how to activate" hint, same role .hero-btn-icon plays
   inline with the "Select" label. */
.hero-mute-gp-icon {
    display: none;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
}
/* Floating "press A/Enter" icon for tabs with no hero button (Movies is the
   only one left — Scoreboards/TV Logos got their own split-preview hero
   panel alongside Stadiums/Kits, see cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp's showSplit) —
   RmlMenu_Sync positions it over whichever row is hovered (mouse) or,
   failing that, the keyboard/gamepad-selected row. */
#row-action-hint {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
}
/* L/R (gamepad) or Left/Right (keyboard) tab-switch hints, flanking the tab
   strip itself rather than living in the bottom hint bar. */
#tab-hint-l, #tab-hint-r {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
}
#filter-btn, #filter-clear-btn {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
}
#filter-btn:hover, #filter-clear-btn:hover { background-color: #FFFFFF26; }
/* Loading spinner, shown over the list area in place of the (stale) row
   list while Python's _update_menu_content() is still computing a new one
   (see RmlOverlay_MenuLoading — set around any wizard-step/tab navigation,
   most usefully for the Stadiums tab's filesystem scan). No conic-gradient/
   mask support in this renderer (see the codebase's other gradient-decorator
   comments), so this is the classic pre-conic-gradient technique instead: a
   full ring plus one differently-colored side, spun with `animation` (a
   real RCSS feature — RmlUi's own Samples/basic/animation/data/animation.rml
   uses the identical from/to rotate() keyframe pair for its own spinner). */
#loading-spinner {
    display: none;
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 22px;
    border: 4px #FFFFFF26;
    border-top-color: #00F2FE;
    animation: 0.9s linear-out infinite spin;
}
@keyframes spin {
    from { transform: rotate(0deg); }
    to { transform: rotate(360deg); }
}
.hint-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 22px;
    position: absolute;
}
.hint-item {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    align-items: center;
    column-gap: 5px;
    padding: 3px 10px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    background-color: #FFFFFF0D;
    /* The bottom Navigate/Scroll/Close hint row items never get an explicit
       pixel width from C++ (only their container row does — RmlMenu_Sync's
       SetRectPx(g_hintKeyRow/g_hintGpRow, ...)), so content-box vs
       border-box never mattered for them; they just auto-size to their
       content. #filter-btn/#filter-clear-btn DO get an explicit SetRectPx
       width, though, and under content-box (RmlUi's default) this class's
       own padding (3px 10px) added on top of that width rather than eating
       into it — the same pitfall #split-preview's own box-sizing comment
       above already documents for this file. That silently grew each
       button ~20px wider than RmlMenu_Sync's math assumed, which was
       exactly enough to make them overlap each other and hang off the
       panel's right edge. */
    box-sizing: border-box;
}
/* "Close" hint (Esc/B) is also a real clickable button (C++ side adds a
   Click listener on hintkey2/hintgp2 — see g_hintCloseKey/g_hintCloseGp in
   cgfs16_rmlui_menu.cpp) — same look as every other hint item at rest, only
   a hover highlight added so it reads as interactive, same treatment as
   .row:hover elsewhere in this file. */
.hint-item.hint-close {
    transition: background-color 0.2s sine-in-out;
}
.hint-item.hint-close:hover {
    background-color: #FFFFFF26;
}
.hint-item .icons {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: row;
    column-gap: 3px;
}
.hint-item img {
    display: block;
    width: 32px;
    height: 32px;
}
.hint-item .label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 14px;
    color: #8B9BB4FF;
    white-space: nowrap;
    /* Safety net for #filter-clear-btn's longer gamepad-mode text ("Hold to
       Clear" vs "Clear") — nowrap text doesn't shrink to fit a flex
       container on its own, so without this it would spill past the
       button's own (now border-box-correct, see .hint-item) right edge
       instead of just being clipped. */
    overflow: hidden;
    text-overflow: ellipsis;
}
</style>
</head>
